UNUSED files (config.h.in, zig.h, wasi.c) are vendored only to keep the
import a faithful, auditable snapshot of what upstream stage1 ships. The
minimal `wasm2c.c` translator here emits fully self-contained C (its own
load/store/convert helpers are written into the output), so the generated
`coilc.c` does NOT include `zig.h`, and our runtime does NOT use WASI.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Modifications we made (kept as SEPARATE git commits after this pristine
import, so the diff from upstream stays auditable)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Coil's `coilc.wasm` is a **memory64** module (i64 linear-memory addresses)
that also uses the **bulk-memory** proposal (memory.copy / memory.fill).
Upstream stage1 wasm2c targets only wasm32 MVP and rejects these. Our
changes are the minimal, correct handling required to translate it:

  InputStream.h
    * InputStream_readLimits(): accept the 64-bit limit flag bytes 0x04 /
      0x05 (memory64 & table64) in addition to the MVP 0x00 / 0x01. The
      min/max LEB values are read unchanged (they are ordinary unsigned
      LEBs regardless of index width).

  wasm2c.c
    * evalExpr()   (constant-offset evaluator for elem/data segments):
      also accept i64.const (0x42), needed because memory64 active
      segments encode their offset as i64.const.
    * renderExpr() (constant initialiser for globals): also render
      i64.const (0x42) as UINT64_C(...), needed for the module's i64
      globals (__heap_base, __stack_pointer, GOT.* ...).
    * table parsing: allow a table whose limits have no maximum (flag
      0x04). Such a table cannot grow without table.grow (which this
      module never uses), so it is sized to its declared minimum. Only
      the min==max panic is relaxed; sizing already used the minimum.
    * export handling: emit an accessor for exported GLOBALS (export kind
      0x03) as `TYPE *const wasm_<name> = &g<idx>;` so the C runtime can
      read exports such as __heap_base. (Upstream handled only function
      and memory exports.)

  Note on memory64 loads/stores: NO change was needed. Each memory access
  the translator emits indexes the linear-memory buffer with the address
  operand popped off the value stack, e.g. `m0[l7 + UINT32_C(off)]`. For a
  memory64 module that operand is an i64 value, so its C local is declared
  `uint64_t` (via WasmValType_toC), and the index arithmetic is naturally
  64-bit. call_indirect likewise indexes the function table with an i64
  local for table64. bulk-memory memory.copy/memory.fill were already
  translated by upstream (to memmove/memset); they too Just Work with i64
  operands. memory.grow / memory.size are never used by this module.

